[5] For thousands of years, astronomical observations relied mainly on visible light, which occupies only a small part of the electromagnetic spectrum. Advances in radio physics made radio astronomy another means of observation and broadened our understanding of the universe.
Observations in different radio bands complement visible-light observations and help investigate celestial objects that were previously difficult to detect or understand. Neutrinos provide a carrier of astronomical information in addition to electromagnetic radiation.
