[7] Earth's atmosphere absorbs electromagnetic radiation from many parts of the spectrum, so ground-based observations use particular bands that can pass through it. Sending instruments beyond the atmosphere provides information that is difficult to receive at the surface.
Space observations extend ultraviolet, infrared, $x$ ray and $\gamma$ ray astronomy and allow the study of primary cosmic rays before they interact with the atmosphere. Different instruments detect different bands and particles. This does not mean that every kind of particle and radiation can already be observed.
These observations have greatly expanded astronomy's sources of information, with an important contribution from detection methods and recording instruments developed in physics. Combining astronomy with physics allows astrophysics to investigate the properties and processes of celestial objects more deeply.
