[8] Molecular biology and genetics investigate the molecular basis of life processes and the transmission and action of genetic information. Molecules are material components of living systems, not tiny organisms that live independently.
Researchers use electron microscopy and charged-particle-beam methods, $x$ ray structural analysis, electron imaging, neutron analysis, isotope tracing and ultracentrifugation to observe, separate or investigate biological samples. These methods have different domains of use and are not all the same kind of microscopic imaging.
They use physical principles such as interactions of particles or radiation with matter, radioactivity and rotational motion. Theories, instruments and experimental methods from physics work together with biological and chemical research to deepen our understanding of life processes.
