[11] Nuclear energy gives energy engineering a pathway distinct from burning chemical fuels. For equal masses, complete fission of fissile material can release several million times as much energy as burning coal;
this is not a ratio of the actual electricity output or efficiency of two power stations.
    break: {"before":"Physics also provides a scientific foundation for many technological developments. Energy engineering, communication, transport, construction and industrial and agricultural production can use physical laws and research findings to improve equipment and processes.\nSuch improvements also require contributions from other disciplines and engineering practice.","after":"Nuclear energy gives energy engineering a pathway distinct from burning chemical fuels. For equal masses, complete fission of fissile material can release several million times as much energy as burning coal;\nthis is not a ratio of the actual electricity output or efficiency of two power stations."}
[12] The potential energy of nuclear-fuel resources depends on usable materials, fuel cycles and technological conditions. Underground reserves alone do not establish that practically available nuclear energy must exceed the energy available from fossil fuels.
Coal, oil and natural gas serve both as fuels and as raw materials for the chemical industry, and burning large quantities consumes these finite resources. Uranium can be used as nuclear fuel, and thorium also has potential in suitable nuclear fuel cycles, but their use is not free of resource or environmental costs.
High energy density is an important characteristic of nuclear energy. Its practical use must also consider safety, radioactive waste and resource management.
